{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n1le01ob10re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n1le01ob10re2aud01", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob10re2img01.jpg", "text": "<p><strong>Sara</strong>: Juan, what are you doing? Are you studying for the quiz?<br /><strong>Juan</strong>: No, I am not studying. What are you talking about? What quiz?<br /><strong>Sara</strong>: The final one. Laura and I are reviewing our notes about object oriented programming. We aren't checking complex ideas to understand the concept. Instead, we're trying to link its definitions with everyday examples and simple general concepts. <br /><strong>Juan</strong>: Can you explain that to me? <br /><strong>Sara</strong>: Yes! Basically, object oriented programming is an approach to software development, which uses objects to solve different problems.", "time": "0"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob10re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Juan</strong>: Objects? <br /><strong>Sara</strong>: Objects are its basic units. They are components that compose the general performance of the program. It means that the object knows how the actions of any program work and how they interact with each other.<br /><strong>Juan</strong>: Any example? <br /><strong>Sara</strong>: Sure. Because we aren't studying from books, let's think about an everyday example. Mmm, a person! What is the first thing you expect a person to have? <br /><strong>Juan</strong>: A name, right?", "time": "41000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob10re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Sara</strong>: Good! A name is to a person what objects are to OOP; That is the property of an object. In addition, you can also expect the person to have a certain skill or ability, such as writing or singing; that is, in terms of software programming, the functionalities or methods that an object has. Now, to create codes around those objects, you need to have in mind some steps: First, we need to understand the components of the object we want to create; second, we need to describe them, and finally, you need to bring them together so they can interact and fulfill the result you want. For example, I'm creating a program whose function is to lead a person who walks from point A to point B. First, you describe the way and the person separately; next, you include the methods, and to conclude, you join them together so the person can walk on that road from A to B.</p>", "time": "76000"}]}}], "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can talk about the concept of object oriented programming. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about objects as components of a program.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can use an everyday example to talk about object oriented programming.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Why do you think object oriented programming is important?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Can you think of an example that shows what an object in programming is?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "An Approach to Software Development", "type": "free_type_text_area"}]}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "STI", "stimuli_i